---
/**
 * website/src/pages/changelog.astro — the changelog (website plan, W1.9).
 *
 * The page is an index of `docs/releases/vX.Y.Z.md`: the hand-written Highlights of the newest
 * releases, newest first, rendered from the files themselves. Five things follow from that
 * and are worth stating here rather than in the component:
 *
 *   1. **The entries are built during the build, and nothing is committed.** W0.6's
 *      `src/lib/changelog.mjs` reads the release files and this page renders what it
 *      returns, so a Highlights file that lands on `main` before its tag exists is on the
 *      page at the next build and there is no generated copy in the tree to go stale
 *      (D100). The page is also the first reader-facing index of those files: `README.md`
 *      deliberately keeps no changelog section and points at the Releases page instead
 *      (finding 44).
 *   2. **The page writes nothing of its own.** Its heading names it, the component renders
 *      each release's end-user summary from `website/data/release-summaries.yaml` and then
 *      the release file's own lines below it, and `website/test/changelog-page.mjs` fails on
 *      a sentence that is in neither: the page-level form of W0.6's file-level check. Both
 *      halves of an entry are text a file of this repository carries, which is what lets
 *      them exist at all.
 *   3. **The repository the entries link is a fact.** It comes from `website/data/site.yaml`
 *      through the content pipeline (W0.2, W1.3), so the release URLs are built from the
 *      site's own fact and no host is typed into a template.
 *   4. **Three releases, and the whole history one link away** (D1640, D1648). The files stay
 *      the source of an entry, and the page renders the three newest of them: a reader who
 *      wants the rest is sent to GitHub's releases page, which is where every one of them is
 *      stated with its date — and which is also where the console's releases are, beside them.
 *      Every release still needs its summary line whether or not the page shows it (F15,
 *      D1647), which is why the check below runs over every release the generator returned —
 *      a file above the CLI's current version is the next release's notes, so it is not one.
 *   5. **The two streams beside the CLI are one line each** (D1641, D1644): the desktop
 *      console's latest release, and the phone app's when it has one. Both are read from the
 *      notes directories and package manifests of those streams (`src/lib/console-release.mjs`,
 *      `src/lib/mobile-release.mjs`) at build time, so no version is typed here; the app's
 *      sentence is its own summaries file, because the app publishes no GitHub release.
 *
 * Nothing here fetches anything: the build is network-free and reads this repository only
 * (Global Constraints 4 and 5).
 *
 * **The page's own look is the bold one, and it is the owner's brief of 2026-10-21** — the
 * same look the owner approved on the home page (`website/design-ref/HomeV3.dc.html`,
 * `src/pages/index.astro`) applied to this page, which has no board of its own. So the page
 * opens with the display scale's largest step: `Changelog` at the weight, the tracking and the
 * leading the boards set a headline in (`base.css`'s `.display-xl`), its word in the accent's
 * gradient (`.gradient-text`, the same class the hero's last two words are drawn in), with the
 * room under it a page's first block gets. **The page adds no words to do it**: the headline is
 * still the page's one `h1` and the only text above the first release, which is why the page
 * has no eyebrow, no pill and no lead paragraph — every sentence below it is a release file's, a
 * release summary's, or one of the words the plan allows the page to write for itself (the link
 * to the full list, and the console's and the app's line), and `website/test/changelog-page.mjs`
 * refuses a sentence that is none of them. The entries under it are the cards `Changelog.astro`
 * draws.
 */
import path from 'node:path'
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import Changelog from '../components/Changelog.astro'
import { buildChangelog } from '../lib/changelog.mjs'
import { latestConsoleRelease } from '../lib/console-release.mjs'
import { latestMobileRelease } from '../lib/mobile-release.mjs'
import { loadMobileReleaseSummaries, loadReleaseSummaries, loadSite, REPO_ROOT } from '../content.ts'

// The releases, read from docs/releases/ at build time (W0.6): newest first, each entry
// the file's own tag, bullets and operator steps, and its `released` flag from the plugin's
// own version (the owner's brief of 2026-10-19) — never from the checkout's tags, which a
// deploy build does not carry.
//
// **Why the two files are passed rather than defaulted.** The generator finds the repository
// by walking up from its own file, which is right when it is loaded from `website/src/lib/`
// — as W0.6's own test loads it — and wrong inside a build: Astro bundles it into a chunk
// under `website/dist/.prerender/`, where that walk lands on `website/` and the release
// directory it looks for is `website/docs/releases/`, which does not exist.
// `website/src/content.ts` solves exactly this problem for the site's facts
// (`findSiteRoot`, W1.3) and exports the repository root it found, so the page passes the
// directory of release files and the plugin manifest the generator should read, and the
// generator itself is left as W0.6 wrote it.
const releasesDir = path.join(REPO_ROOT, 'docs', 'releases')
const pluginFile = path.join(REPO_ROOT, 'plugin', '.claude-plugin', 'plugin.json')
const streamsFile = path.join(REPO_ROOT, 'docs', 'release-streams.yaml')
const entries = buildChangelog({ releasesDir, pluginFile, streamsFile })

// The end-user summaries below each release's heading: the per-release lines the owner's
// brief of 2026-10-19 asked for, read from the site's own data through the content pipeline
// (W1.3) so the page writes no prose of its own. The component renders the summary it finds
// and stops the build when a release has none.
const summaries = loadReleaseSummaries()

// **Every release needs its line, and not only the ones below the cut** (F15, D1647).
// The page renders the three newest releases and links the rest to GitHub, so a check that ran
// over the rendered entries alone would let an older release lose its line without saying
// so — and the line is the one thing that file has on this site. So the check runs here, over
// every entry the generator returned, before the cut below — the generator's entries are the
// releases, the drafts above the CLI's current version having been left out there. The
// component holds the rendered entries to the same rule while it renders them.
for (const entry of entries) {
  if (summaries[entry.tag] === undefined) {
    throw new Error(
      `docs/releases/${entry.tag}.md has no entry in website/data/release-summaries.yaml, and every release file needs one: the changelog renders that file's line under the release's heading, for the whole directory and not only the three entries it shows (F15, D1647, the owner's brief of 2026-10-19).`,
    )
  }
}

// The three newest releases, which is what the page renders (D1640). The generator still
// returns every release file, because the home page's hero reads the newest published entry
// out of the same call (F10) — the cut is this page's, and it is a cut rather than a filter:
// the older entries are on GitHub, and the link under them says where (D1648).
const SHOWN = 3
const shown = entries.slice(0, SHOWN)

// The two streams beside the CLI (D1641, D1644). Each stream's latest release is the newest
// notes file at or below its own package's version — `src/lib/console-release.mjs` and
// `src/lib/mobile-release.mjs`, the rule both were written to apply. The console's line names
// its version and links its GitHub release; the app publishes no GitHub release, so its line
// is the sentence its own summaries file carries for that release, and a release it has
// published with no line there stops the build rather than rendering a line with nothing to
// say. Both streams are read at build time, so no version is typed into this page.
const consoleRelease = latestConsoleRelease({
  notesDir: path.join(releasesDir, 'console'),
  packageFile: path.join(REPO_ROOT, 'console', 'package.json'),
})
const appRelease = latestMobileRelease({
  notesDir: path.join(releasesDir, 'mobile'),
  packageFile: path.join(REPO_ROOT, 'mobile', 'package.json'),
})
const appSummaries = loadMobileReleaseSummaries()
let mobileRelease: { version: string; tag: string; summary: string } | null = null
if (appRelease !== null) {
  const summary = appSummaries[appRelease.tag]
  if (summary === undefined) {
    throw new Error(
      `docs/releases/mobile/v${appRelease.version}.md is the app's latest release and website/data/mobile-release-summaries.yaml carries no ${appRelease.tag} entry, and the changelog renders that file's line as the app's line (D1644).`,
    )
  }
  mobileRelease = { version: appRelease.version, tag: appRelease.tag, summary }
}

// The site's facts, through the same pipeline every page renders from: the repository is
// the one fact this page needs, and `loadSite()` fails the build if the facts are not
// there rather than rendering a page with a hole in it (W1.3).
const repository = loadSite().facts.repository
---

<Base title="Changelog">
  {/* The page's head: the headline, and the room under it. `header` inside `main` is not a
      landmark (a banner is a `header` of the body, and `website/test/a11y.mjs` counts the
      page-level ones), so it is a box and nothing more. */}
  <header class="changelog-head">
    {/* The boards' background: the grid and the two soft glows behind the top of a page,
        drawn by `base.css`'s `.hero-glow` — the class the home page's hero opens with, used
        here rather than a second drawing of the same thing. It is decoration and says so
        (`aria-hidden`), and it paints no text. */}
    <div class="hero-glow" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <h1 class="changelog-title display-xl">
      <span class="gradient-text">Changelog</span>
    </h1>
  </header>
  <Changelog
    entries={shown}
    summaries={summaries}
    repository={repository}
    consoleRelease={consoleRelease}
    mobileRelease={mobileRelease}
  />
</Base>

<style>
  /* The page's head, in the page's own stylesheet: the headline, the room under it and the
     boards' background behind it. **The room above it is `main`'s own padding and nothing
     else** — the top of the block is `0`, so this page's headline sits at the offset every
     other page's headline sits at (`main` is `--space-lg` there and `--space-xl` from 48 rem
     up, `base.css`), and the gutter is `main`'s too. Only the step between the headline and
     the first card is this block's, and it is a step of the spacing scale rather than a
     number. The pages whose first block is a full container of its own — the home page's hero,
     the security document — drop `main`'s padding instead (`main:has(> .hero, >
     [data-security-page])`); this head is not one of those, so it keeps `main`'s padding and
     adds no second one on top of it. `position: relative` and `overflow: hidden` are what the
     glow layer needs (`base.css`, section 2c). */
  .changelog-head {
    position: relative;
    overflow: hidden;
    padding-block: 0 var(--space-2xl);
  }

  /* The headline paints above the glow: a positioned box paints after a static one, and the
     glow is the head's first child (the hero's copy is positioned for the same reason). */
  .changelog-title {
    position: relative;
    margin: 0;
    /* The headline is one word, and the display scale's balance (`text-wrap: balance`) has
       nothing to balance: it is set on one line at every width it fits, which the clamp in
       `.display-xl` is what decides. */
    max-width: 20ch;
  }
</style>
